  "question": {
    "text": "If an interference experiment is performed using two wavelengths close to each other, two interference patterns corresponding to the two wavelengths are obtained on the screen. The fringe system remains distinct up to a point on the screen where the&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup></math> order maximum of one wavelength falls on the&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup></math> order minimum of the other wavelength. Suppose in Young's double sit experiment, sodium light composed of two wavelengths <math><msub><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math> and <math><msub><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></math> close to each other ( with <math><msub><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></math> greater than <math><msub><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math> ) is used. The order <math><mi>n</mi></math>, up to which the fringes can be seen on the screen, is given by"
